        <div id="cfm-and-static-pressure" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. CFM and Static Pressure: The Numbers That Matter
          </h2>

          <p>
            CFM is the volume of air a collector moves, and it is what carries chips away from the cutter. Most woodworking guidance suggests a 4-inch port on a table saw or planer needs several hundred CFM at the tool to capture well, which is why 1 HP collectors rated around 650 to 800 CFM are the practical minimum for one machine.
          </p>

          <p>
            The catch is that rated CFM is measured with nothing attached. Every foot of hose, every elbow and every filter adds resistance, called static pressure, and airflow drops as resistance climbs. A collector that publishes airflow at a stated static pressure, like Grizzly's 1,654 CFM at 2.0 inches for the G0441, tells you far more than a single open-air number.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Model</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Rated CFM</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Static Pressure</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Filtration</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Jet DC-1100VX-5M</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,100</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10.5 in. (max)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5 micron bag</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Grizzly G0441</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,654 at 2.0 in. SP</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">14.2 in. (max)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0.2-2 micron cartridge</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Shop Fox W1727</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">800</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5.67 in.</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2.5 micron bag</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">WEN DC3401</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">660</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not published in our sources</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5 micron per reviews</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Central Machinery 2 HP</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,550 (disputed)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not published in our sources</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5 micron upper bag</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Oneida Supercell Turbo</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Up to 536</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">97.8 in.</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">HEPA-grade</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Use static pressure to compare collectors you plan to duct. A higher maximum static pressure means the fan holds its airflow better against long runs. The Jet's 10.5 inches and the Grizzly's 14.2 inches handle ducting well; the Shop Fox's 5.67 inches is fine for a short hose but will fade on a long run.
          </p>

          <p>
            Be skeptical of outliers. Forum users dispute the Central Machinery's 1,550 CFM rating, and the general rule holds: a collector with a small impeller and a modest motor does not outflow larger machines just because its box says so.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="filtration" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Filtration and Fine Dust
          </h2>

          <p>
            Chips are a mess; fine dust is the health problem. The particles small enough to stay airborne after you stop cutting are the ones that reach deep into the lungs, and a 5-micron bag lets a large share of them straight back into the room. This is the single most common weakness of budget collectors.
          </p>

          <p>
            Filter ratings tell you what you are buying. The Jet and WEN use 5-micron bags, the Shop Fox a 2.5-micron bag, and the Grizzly G0441 a cartridge rated 0.2 to 2 microns. Grizzly's G0441HEP adds a HEPA stage rated 99.97 percent at 0.3 microns, and Oneida describes the Supercell's filter as HEPA-grade.
          </p>

          <p>
            Upgrading is common and worthwhile. Owners of the Central Machinery unit routinely swap the 5-micron bag for a 1-micron bag or a pleated canister, and the same upgrade is available for most single-stage collectors. A canister also has far more surface area than a bag, which keeps airflow higher between cleanings.
          </p>

          <p>
            Even a good collector does not catch everything. Run an ambient air filter during and after sanding, and wear a respirator for the dustiest work. Collection, air filtration and a mask together are what keep a shop genuinely clean.
          </p>

          <p>
            Filter maintenance is part of the filtration story. A bag or cartridge loaded with fine dust restricts airflow as surely as a long duct run, so the collector that performed well on day one can feel weak a few months later. Shake out bags, use the paddle or pulse cleaner on canisters where fitted, and empty the collection bag or drum before it is full, because a packed bag also blocks airflow. Follow the maker's guidance on cleaning method, since some filter media should not be blown out with compressed air.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="single-vs-two-stage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Single-Stage or Cyclone
          </h2>

          <p>
            A single-stage collector pulls chips through the impeller and blows them into a bag, with the filter bag on top. It is simple, compact and affordable, which is why the Jet, Shop Fox, WEN and Central Machinery units use it. The downsides are that every chip passes through the fan and fine dust loads the filter quickly.
          </p>

          <p>
            A two-stage collector, usually a cyclone, spins the air so heavy chips fall into a drum before the air reaches the fan or filter. The Grizzly G0441 and Oneida Supercell work this way. Filters stay cleaner far longer, airflow stays more consistent, and an accidental chunk of wood hits the drum rather than the impeller.
          </p>

          <p>
            Jet's Vortex Cone on the DC-1100VX is a middle ground: a cone inside the single-stage unit that helps drop chips out before the filter. Many owners also add a separate cyclone separator in front of a single-stage collector, which gets much of the two-stage benefit for a fraction of the cost.
          </p>

          <p>
            Choose a cyclone if you run several chip-heavy machines, mill a lot of rough lumber or want minimal filter maintenance. Choose single-stage if you run one machine at a time in a small shop and value floor space and price.
          </p>

          <p>
            Space is the practical tiebreaker. A single-stage unit like the Shop Fox W1727 rolls into a corner, while a cyclone such as the G0441 stands tall with a drum underneath and is usually bolted to a wall or stand. Measure your ceiling height and the path to empty the drum before you commit to a cyclone, especially in a basement shop.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ducting" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Hoses, Ducting and Blast Gates
          </h2>

          <p>
            How you connect the collector matters as much as which collector you buy. Flexible hose has far more resistance per foot than smooth rigid duct, so keep flex runs short and use rigid duct for the main line in a fixed installation. Wide sweeping elbows lose far less airflow than tight 90-degree bends.
          </p>

          <p>
            Size the main line to the collector. The Grizzly G0441's 8-inch inlet is designed for a large main that branches down to tools; choking it with a 4-inch main throws away much of its capacity. Smaller collectors with 4-inch inlets are best used with a single hose moved from tool to tool.
          </p>

          <p>
            Install blast gates at each machine and open only the one you are using. A collector pulling through every open drop at once delivers little to any of them. Many owners add remote or automatic switches so the collector starts with the tool.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ground or bond your ducting as the manufacturer recommends and keep the system clean. Fine dust accumulating in ducts and filters is both a performance and a housekeeping problem, and the manual for your collector will cover its specific requirements.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plan the layout on paper before you buy fittings. Sketch where each machine sits, run the shortest practical main line past them, and count the elbows and transitions you will need. Each fitting you can remove is airflow you keep, and planning the run first usually shows whether a portable collector on a single hose would serve you better than a fixed system.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="power-and-placement" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Power, Placement and Noise
          </h2>

          <p>
            Check the electrical needs before you buy. The Shop Fox W1727 runs on standard 110V and the Jet DC-1100VX runs on 115V or 230V, but the Grizzly G0441 needs 220V and a 22-amp dedicated circuit, and the Oneida Supercell Turbo needs a 230V 30-amp circuit. Adding a circuit can be a significant part of the cost.
          </p>

          <p>
            Placement affects both airflow and noise. Put the collector close to the machines that make the most chips, usually the planer and table saw, to keep the longest runs short. Some owners place a large collector in a closet or adjacent space to cut noise, which works as long as the air returns to the shop.
          </p>

          <p>
            Noise listings run from about 70 dB on the Jet to 83 to 85 dB on the standard Grizzly G0441, with the HEPA version listed at 79 to 80 dB. Wear hearing protection when running any collector alongside a planer or saw.
          </p>

          <p>
            Leave room to empty the bag or drum. A 55-gallon drum full of chips is heavy, and a bag hung against a wall is awkward to change. Plan the location around the chore you will do most often.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Which Dust Collector to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            For a typical garage shop running one big tool at a time, the Jet DC-1100VX-5M is the pick. It has the airflow and static pressure to handle a short duct run, runs on standard power, and leaves room to upgrade the filter later.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a small shop or a tight budget, the Shop Fox W1727 is a solid first collector with a finer 2.5-micron bag, and the WEN DC3401 is the cheapest real step up from a shop vacuum for chip capture at one machine. If you are comfortable upgrading filters, the Central Machinery 2 HP gives you a lot of collector for very little money.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a dedicated, ducted shop with several machines, the Grizzly G0441 or its HEPA version is the serious system. For fine dust from sanders, routers and CNC machines, the Oneida Supercell is the premium specialist. If you are kitting out a shop from scratch, our <a href="/best-band-saw/">band saw guide</a> and <a href="/best-table-saw/">table saw guide</a> cover the machines these collectors serve.
          </p>

          <p>
            The same lesson applies outdoors: rated airflow and real airflow differ. Our <a href="/best-backpack-leaf-blower/">backpack leaf blower guide</a> covers the housing-versus-nozzle CFM trap that catches buyers of high-airflow yard tools too.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting open-air CFM</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Rated CFM is measured with nothing attached. Hose, elbows and filters cut it substantially. Compare collectors on airflow at a stated static pressure where the maker publishes it, and expect less at the tool than on the box.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a shop vac on a planer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Shop vacuums move too little air for chip-heavy machines with 4-inch ports. A planer or jointer needs a dust collector; save the vacuum for sanders and other small-port tools.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the 5-micron bag on</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 5-micron bag returns much of the finest dust to the room. Upgrade to a 1-micron bag or canister where available, and run an air filter and wear a respirator for sanding.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running all blast gates open</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A collector pulling through every open drop delivers little airflow anywhere. Fit blast gates and open only the machine you are using.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the electrical requirement</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Large collectors need 220V and a dedicated circuit; the Grizzly G0441 is listed at 22 amps and the Oneida Turbo needs 30 amps. Price the circuit before you buy the collector.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size dust collector do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For one machine at a time with a short hose, a 1 HP collector rated around 650 to 800 CFM is the practical minimum. A 1.5 to 2 HP unit suits a garage shop with short ducting, and a 3 HP cyclone suits a ducted shop with several machines.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a dust collector better than a shop vac?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For different jobs. A dust collector moves high volume at low pressure, ideal for planers and table saws with 4-inch ports. A shop vacuum moves low volume at high pressure, ideal for small ports on sanders and handheld tools. Most shops benefit from both.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What micron filter should a dust collector have?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Finer is better for health. Many budget bags are 5 microns, which lets fine dust escape. A 1-micron bag or canister is a common upgrade, and HEPA filters like Grizzly's G0441HEP stage are rated 99.97 percent at 0.3 microns.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a cyclone dust collector worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If you run several chip-heavy machines or want minimal filter maintenance, yes. A cyclone drops chips into a drum before they reach the filter, keeping airflow consistent. For one tool at a time in a small shop, a single-stage unit is usually enough.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a dust collector run on 110V?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many smaller units can. The Shop Fox W1727 is prewired for 110V and the Jet DC-1100VX runs on 115V or 230V. Larger collectors like the Grizzly G0441 need 220V and a dedicated circuit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the Harbor Freight dust collector any good?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is popular value for money, but forum users dispute its 1,550 CFM rating and recommend upgrading its 5-micron bag to a 1-micron filter. Owners who accept those upgrades often consider it excellent value for the price.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How far can a dust collector pull through ducting?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on static pressure and duct design. Higher-pressure units like the Jet at 10.5 inches handle longer runs than lower-pressure units. Oneida rates the Supercell for runs up to 100 feet. Keep flex hose short and use rigid duct for main lines.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

